---
import StudyLayout from '../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/study/Section.astro';
import Table from '../../components/study/Table.astro';
import Milestones from '../../components/study/Milestones.astro';
import Bios from '../../components/study/Bios.astro';
import { PAPERS, PHIL, ETHICS, CHRISTIAN, MILESTONES, LEVELS } from '../../data/study/rs';
import { byTag } from '../../data/study/people';
import type { Topic } from '../../data/study/types';

const TopicGrid = (items: Topic[]) => items;
---
<StudyLayout
  title="Religious Studies"
  eyebrow="OCR H573 · three papers · no coursework"
  lead="Thirty topics, one essay form. Each paper sets four essays and she answers three, forty marks and forty minutes each. Argument is worth more than knowledge in every essay, so a well-argued essay with adequate knowledge beats a knowledgeable essay that describes."
  current="rs"
>
  <Section title="The exam" intro="Each essay is 16 marks AO1 (knowledge and understanding) and 24 marks AO2 (analysis and evaluation), marked on a six-level grid. Questions can come from any topic, so nothing is dropped.">
    <Table head={PAPERS.head} rows={PAPERS.rows} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="The method" intro="Each topic takes about ten days, alternating papers so she does not spend a term inside one way of thinking.">
    <div class="study-prose">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Read the Hodder chapter</strong> (about 20 pages) in the reading slot, making a two-page summary in her own words.</li>
        <li><strong>Build the topic sheet:</strong> one A4 page. Key ideas in the left column, criticisms and counter-criticisms in the middle, named scholars with one quotable sentence each on the right. It must fit on one page.</li>
        <li><strong>The for-and-against table:</strong> for each of the two or three obvious questions on the topic, three arguments each way and a judgement.</li>
        <li><strong>Write one 40-mark essay in 40 minutes.</strong> Not a plan. The whole essay, timed, marked the same day.</li>
        <li><strong>Read a model essay</strong> (Baron's book) on the same or a neighbouring question afterwards, never before. Note two things the model did that hers did not.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>The one essay form she needs.</strong> A four-sentence introduction: define the key term, state the thesis, name the thinkers. Three or four paragraphs, each with a claim, the scholar or argument that supports it, the strongest objection, her response to the objection, and a sentence back to the question. A four-sentence conclusion: the judgement, the decisive reason, the strongest opposing point and why it fails.</p>
      <p>The single habit that lifts AO2 from level 4 to level 6 is answering the objection inside the paragraph. Most students present view A, then view B, then conclude. Examiners want her to weigh them mid-paragraph.</p>
      <p><strong>Links page</strong> for synoptic credit: Natural Law to Augustine; Kant to Christian moral principles; religious experience to knowledge of God; problem of evil to death and afterlife; Hick on evil and Hick on pluralism; Freud on conscience and Freud on secularism. One sentence drawing a link is worth having in every essay.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Philosophy of Religion (H573/01)" intro="Nine topics. Taught in Terms 1, 2, 3 and 4.">
    <div class="study-grid">
      {PHIL.map((t) => (
        <article class="study-card"><p class="study-card__kicker">{t.weeks}</p><h3 class="study-card__title">{t.title}</h3><p class="study-card__body">{t.intro}</p></article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Religion and Ethics (H573/02)" intro="Nine topics. Meta-ethics is taught early because it sharpens every other essay.">
    <div class="study-grid">
      {ETHICS.map((t) => (
        <article class="study-card"><p class="study-card__kicker">{t.weeks}</p><h3 class="study-card__title">{t.title}</h3><p class="study-card__body">{t.intro}</p></article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Developments in Christian Thought (H573/03)" intro="Twelve topics. Begins in Term 3 and finishes in Term 5.">
    <div class="study-grid">
      {CHRISTIAN.map((t) => (
        <article class="study-card"><p class="study-card__kicker">{t.weeks}</p><h3 class="study-card__title">{t.title}</h3><p class="study-card__body">{t.intro}</p></article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Essay titles by topic" intro="One full essay per topic, in the week shown. The milestones below are the ones marked with the full grid and logged.">
    <Table head={['Weeks', 'Topic', 'Essay written']} rows={[
      ['1 to 2', 'Phil 1: Plato and Aristotle', '"Plato’s theory of Forms is more convincing than Aristotle’s account of reality." Discuss.'],
      ['2 to 3', 'Ethics 1: Natural Law', '"Natural Law is the best approach to ethical decision-making." Discuss.'],
      ['4 to 5', 'Phil 2: Soul, mind and body', '"Dualism is a better account of the mind than materialism." Discuss.'],
      ['5 to 6', 'Ethics 2: Situation Ethics', '<span class="ms">M1</span> "Agape is not a sufficient basis for ethics." Discuss.'],
      ['7 to 8', 'Phil 3: Arguments from observation', '"The teleological argument fails because of Hume’s criticisms." Discuss.'],
      ['8 to 9', 'Phil 4: Arguments from reason', '"The ontological argument is a valid proof of God’s existence." Discuss.'],
      ['10', 'Review', '<span class="ms">M2</span> two essays from a past Philosophy paper, 80 min'],
      ['11 to 12', 'Ethics 3: Kant', '"Kant’s ethics is too rigid to be useful." Discuss.'],
      ['12 to 13', 'Phil 5: Religious experience', '"Religious experiences are only ever psychological." Discuss.'],
      ['14 to 15', 'Ethics 4: Utilitarianism', '<span class="ms">M3</span> "Utilitarianism is the most practical ethical theory." Discuss.'],
      ['15 to 16', 'Phil 6: Problem of evil', '"Hick’s soul-making theodicy is more successful than Augustine’s." Discuss.'],
      ['17 to 18', 'Ethics 5: Euthanasia', '"Natural Law gives a better response to euthanasia than Situation Ethics." Discuss.'],
      ['19 to 20', 'Ethics 7: Meta-ethics', '"Ethical statements are merely expressions of emotion." Discuss.'],
      ['21', 'Review', '<span class="ms">M4</span> two essays, one per paper, 80 min'],
      ['22 to 23', 'Christian 1: Augustine', '"Augustine’s view of human nature is too pessimistic." Discuss.'],
      ['23 to 24', 'Christian 2: Death and afterlife', '"Hell is incompatible with a loving God." Discuss.'],
      ['25 to 26', 'Christian 3: Knowledge of God', '<span class="ms">M5</span> "Natural theology is a better route to knowledge of God than revealed theology." Discuss.'],
      ['27 to 28', 'Christian 4: Jesus', '"Jesus was primarily a teacher of wisdom." Discuss.'],
      ['29 to 30', 'Ethics 6: Business ethics', '"Kantian ethics is more useful than utilitarianism for business." Discuss.'],
      ['31 to 32', 'Phil 7: Nature of God', '"An eternal God cannot know the future and leave humans free." Discuss.'],
      ['33', 'End-of-year mock', '<span class="ms">M6</span> full Ethics paper, 2 hours'],
      ['35 to 36', 'Phil 8: Religious language', '"Aquinas’ analogy is the best way of talking about God." Discuss.'],
      ['37 to 38', 'Phil 9: Twentieth-century language', '<span class="ms">M7</span> "Religious language is meaningless." Discuss.'],
      ['39 to 41', 'Ethics 8: Conscience', '"Freud’s account of conscience is more convincing than Aquinas’." Discuss.'],
      ['42', 'Ethics 9: Sexual ethics', '"Religious views on sexual ethics are no longer relevant." Discuss.'],
      ['43 to 44', 'Christian 5: Moral principles', '"The Bible is the only authority for Christian ethics." Discuss.'],
      ['45 to 46', 'Christian 6: Bonhoeffer', '<span class="ms">M8</span> "Bonhoeffer’s teaching on obedience has no relevance today." Discuss.'],
      ['49 to 50', 'January mocks', 'Full Philosophy and Ethics papers; half a Christian Thought paper'],
      ['51 to 52', 'Christian 7: Pluralism and theology', '"Hick’s pluralism undermines Christian belief." Discuss.'],
      ['52 to 53', 'Christian 8: Pluralism and society', '"Inter-faith dialogue is essential for Christianity today." Discuss.'],
      ['54 to 55', 'Christian 9 and 10: Gender', '<span class="ms">M9</span> "Christianity cannot be reconciled with feminism." Discuss.'],
      ['56 to 57', 'Christian 11: Secularism', '"Secularism is a threat to Christianity." Discuss.'],
      ['58 to 59', 'Christian 12: Liberation theology', '<span class="ms">M10</span> "Liberation theology has engaged too much with Marx." Discuss.'],
    ]} />
    <p class="study-prose">From April 2028: second mocks in the week of 24 April, then three timed essays a week, one per paper, drawn from every past question OCR has set since 2018 (about 150), tallied so each topic is written on at least twice. By then she should hold a bank of forty ten-bullet essay plans.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Milestones">
    <Milestones items={MILESTONES} />
  </Section>

  <Section title="Marking guide" intro="Mark AO1 and AO2 separately, with the OCR grid.">
    <Table head={LEVELS.head} rows={LEVELS.rows} />
    <p class="study-prose"><strong>Failure points from examiners' reports:</strong> answering the topic rather than the question set; view A, view B, conclusion, with no weighing; conclusions that introduce new material; padding the introduction with biography; scholars' names used as decoration.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Who's who: Philosophy of Religion">
    <Bios people={byTag('philosophy')} />
  </Section>
  <Section title="Who's who: Ethics">
    <Bios people={byTag('ethics').filter((p) => !p.tags?.includes('philosophy'))} />
  </Section>
  <Section title="Who's who: Christian Thought">
    <Bios people={byTag('christian').filter((p) => !p.tags?.includes('philosophy') && !p.tags?.includes('ethics'))} />
  </Section>
</StudyLayout>
